Host          : lilith-river (AMD Ryzen 9 7950X, 128 GiB; CPU has fma+avx2)
Repo          : ~/work/zen/jxl-encoder--w44-recon-deep-a11 (sibling jj
                workspace; parent jxl-encoder)
Parent commit : 05e3bdf0 investigate(W44-recovery): e9 perf profile on 1418519 d=1.0
Fix commit    : (THIS commit) fix(W44-recon-A11): XYB->linear FMA + INV_OPSIN constant
Bench script  : /tmp/w44_a11_ab.py
Source diff   : jxl-encoder-simd/src/xyb.rs (149/-17, 1 file)

WHAT CHANGED
============

W44-RECON-DEEP/A2 (`memory/w44_recon_deep_a2_xyb_linear_path_2026-05-23.md`)
identified two divergences in `inverse_xyb_planar_impl` (and its scalar
fallbacks) vs every libjxl-derived decoder:

1. **Cube+bias FMA association** (~4-5e-6 max-abs linear-RGB worst-case):
   our `g*g*g + neg_bias` had 3 separate roundings; libjxl decoder +
   jxl-rs + jxl-oxide all use `(g*g).mul_add(g, neg_bias)` (single FMA
   on cube+bias). Fixed at 3 sites: `inverse_xyb_planar_scalar`,
   `inverse_xyb_scalar`, `inverse_xyb_planar_impl` (the SIMD body).

2. **INV_OPSIN[0][2] and [1][2] 1-ULP drift** (≤ 1.5e-8 per-pixel):
   our literal `-0.164_623` (bits `0xbe2892ef`) was 1 ULP off from
   libjxl's `-0.16462299647058826f` (bits `0xbe2892ee`). Fixed by
   replacing both literals with the full f64 expression.

A new unit test
`xyb::tests::test_inverse_xyb_libjxl_single_fma_parity` asserts our
output now matches a hand-written libjxl-reference (single-FMA cube+bias
+ full f64 constants) within max-abs 1e-5 across 256 sample XYB triples.
Test passes.

